{"question_id": "06f04340", "question_type": "single-session-preference", "response": "Based on your garden (cherry tomatoes you've already harvested, plus your basil and mint), I'd suggest:\n\n**Main: Pesto Pasta** — basil blended with garlic, pine nuts, Parmesan and olive oil, tossed with linguine, your homegrown cherry tomatoes, and grilled chicken. It uses both your basil and your tomato harvest in one dish.\n\n**Side/starter: Caprese Salad** — fresh mozzarella, your homegrown cherry tomatoes, and basil leaves with olive oil and balsamic glaze. Simple and lets the homegrown tomatoes shine.\n\n**Condiment: Mint Chutney or Minty Yogurt Sauce** — both use your mint; the yogurt sauce (mint, yogurt, cucumber, garlic, dill) works well as a cooling side, and the chutney is great with grilled meats or vegetables.\n\n**Optional drink: Herby Lemonade** — lemonade infused with fresh basil and mint, a nice weekend touch.\n\nIf you'd rather grill, the **Middle Eastern-Style Salad** (basil and mint with bulgur, cherry tomatoes, cucumbers, feta, lemon-tahini dressing) or **Grilled Lamb with Mint Sauce** would also showcase your herbs and tomatoes.\n\nOne note: your pepper plants are still battling aphids (neem oil didn't work), so I'd hold off on featuring peppers until that's resolved — you were considering introducing ladybugs or lacewings, which I think is a great natural approach.", "n_records": 64, "usage": {"prompt_tokens": 5225, "completion_tokens": 1822, "calls": 2}, "selected": ["self|recipe_preferences", "self|cooking_habits", "self|garden_plants", "self|garden_harvest", "self|pest_problem", "self|pest_control_attempts", "self|gardening_interest", "self|pest_control_plans", "assistant|recipe_suggestion", "assistant|companion_plant_suggestion", "assistant|companion_plant_warning", "assistant|gardening_tip", "assistant|pest_control_suggestion", "assistant|pest_control_opinion"], "digest_bytes": 8000}
{"question_id": "38146c39", "question_type": "single-session-preference", "response": "Based on your sugar experiments, here are some ideas drawn from what's worked for you:\n\n- **Try turbinado or muscovado sugar.** You found turbinado adds a richer flavor than other sugars, and muscovado has an even stronger molasses/caramel note. For cookies, the general turbinado tip applies: combine it with other sugars rather than swapping entirely, and adjust liquid slightly since turbinado is drier than muscovado.\n- **Add espresso.** Coffee's bold flavor pairs beautifully with turbinado in mocha desserts — a shot of espresso or a teaspoon of instant coffee deepens the chocolate without making it taste like coffee.\n- **Use darker chocolate.** Cocoa powder or dark chocolate creates a rich, indulgent profile with turbinado sugar, so try dark chocolate chips or a portion of cocoa powder in the dough.\n- **Finish with flaky salt.** A pinch of flaky sea salt on top enhances turbinado's flavor and balances the sweetness.\n- **Add nuts or vanilla.** Nuts like almonds, pecans, or hazelnuts pair well with turbinado, and vanilla's creamy sweetness complements it in cakes — both fit cookies easily.\n\nSince you like stronger, more intense flavors, I'd start with a half-turbinado/half-granulated blend, espresso, dark chocolate chips, and a salt finish.", "n_records": 26, "usage": {"prompt_tokens": 3256, "completion_tokens": 1106, "calls": 2}, "selected": ["self|sugar_experimentation", "self|turbinado_sugar_opinion", "self|carrot_cake_plan", "self|frosting_plan", "self|flavor_preference", "assistant|turbinado_pairing_spices", "assistant|turbinado_pairing_citrus", "assistant|turbinado_pairing_nuts", "assistant|turbinado_pairing_coffee", "assistant|turbinado_pairing_vanilla", "assistant|turbinado_pairing_chocolate", "assistant|turbinado_pairing_dried_fruit", "assistant|turbinado_pairing_coconut", "assistant|turbinado_pairing_ginger", "assistant|turbinado_pairing_salt", "assistant|turbinad